gm-method (a long-running wasm host's dispatches start failing with V8's 'Start offset -<huge> is outside the bounds of the buffer', deterministically, only after a long session, and a fresh process recovers for a while then re-corrupts): ROOT CAUSE is a SIGNED-vs-UNSIGNED pointer cast at the JS<->wasm boundary. A wasm i32 pointer (an alloc return or a host-fn arg) with the high bit set -- a genuine offset > 0x7fffffff, which only occurs once the wasm linear memory has GROWN past ~2GB -- is received in JS as a NEGATIVE number, and new Uint8Array(buffer, negativeOffset, len) / new DataView(buffer, negativeOffset) throws that raw error. The 'after a long session' + 'fresh process recovers then re-corrupts' signature is the tell: it tracks total memory growth, not a specific verb. FIX: coerce EVERY wasm pointer to unsigned with >>>0 (Number(x)>>>0 for BigInt-derived) at EVERY boundary BEFORE using it as a typed-array/DataView offset -- alloc returns most of all (the write-input path blocks dispatches), plus every host-import fn's ptr/len args. Range-guard the offset too (ptr>=0 && ptr+len<=byteLength) so a truly-bad value raises a clean error rather than a raw typed-array throw. The wasm/rust source is usually CORRECT (it passes pointers as u32); the bug lives entirely in the JS wrapper's unsigned cast. Reproduce in a test with any negative offset (negative|0) -- it throws the same 'outside the bounds' -- and assert >>>0 recovers the true offset.